sections.body:
A 30-year-old is stabbed in the back. His right leg is weak with brisk reflexes, and he cannot feel vibration or joint position in the right leg. Pain and temperature are lost in the left leg from just below the umbilicus. This is a right cord hemisection around T9–T10:
- right corticospinal tract (already crossed in the medulla) → right UMN weakness;
- right dorsal columns (uncrossed in the cord) → right loss of vibration and proprioception;
- right spinothalamic tract, carrying fibres that crossed from the left a segment or two below → left loss of pain and temperature beginning slightly below the lesion.
The same logic localises other lesions. A dissociated sensory loss of pain and temperature in a "cape" over the shoulders with preserved touch suggests a central cord lesion such as a syrinx, which damages the crossing fibres in the anterior white commissure. A relative afferent pupillary defect (both pupils dilate when the light swings to the affected eye) indicates optic nerve disease such as optic neuritis, whereas a third nerve palsy impairs the efferent limb so the affected pupil reacts poorly whichever eye is lit.
